<!-- 5. TECHNICAL FORMULATION AND MATERIAL COMPATIBILITY -->
<section class="section" id="5-technical-formulation-and-material-compatibility">
  <h2>5. Technical Formulation and Material Compatibility</h2>
  <p>The active chemical concentrate must be highly compatible with both the aerosol can components (valve, gasket, internal coating) and the target surfaces it will clean. Corrosive disinfectants can degrade elastomer valve gaskets, causing leaks, or cause oxidation on metallic surfaces. Formulators utilize specific buffer systems, corrosion inhibitors, and non-aggressive solvents to ensure product shelf life and substrate safety.</p>
  <p class="intro-para">
                Excellence in disinfectant formulation is achieved at the intersection of potency and gentleness. A high-performance aerosol must kill pathogens without compromising the integrity of medical plastics, stainless steel counters, or the internal gaskets of the aerosol container itself.
            </p>
            <p>
                Compatibility testing is a critical phase of the R&D cycle. For instance, high-alcohol formulations can cause "solvent cracking" in acrylics used in incubators, while oxidative disinfectants can corrode brass or low-grade steel components. Professional formulators use specific corrosion inhibitors and buffer systems to mitigate these risks.
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Gasket and Valve Selection:</strong> Active chemicals like Dimethyl Ether (DME) require specific EPDM or Fluoropolymer gaskets to prevent swelling and failure.</li>
                <li><strong>Can Linings:</strong> Tinplate or Aluminum cans must be internally coated with specialized epoxy-phenolic resins to resist chemical attack from the disinfectant concentrate.</li>
                <li><strong>Active Concentration:</strong> Balancing the "Loading Factor" of the active ingredient (e.g., 0.1% Quats vs 1% Quats) to ensure efficacy while minimizing residue or "filming" on polished surfaces.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

